/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Judges chapters 1-5
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("judges", [
{n:1, name:"The Incomplete Conquest", seal:{ref:"Judges 1:19",verse:"And the LORD was with Judah; and he drave out the inhabitants of the mountain; but could not drive out the inhabitants of the valley, because they had chariots of iron."},
 facts:["Did you know the book of Judges opens with Israel asking God who should fight first, and the answer was Judah? The tribe whose name means praise led the charge into Canaan.",
 "Did you know Adonibezek, the captured Canaanite king, confessed that he had cut off the thumbs and big toes of seventy kings, and admitted God was paying him back in kind?",
 "Did you know Judah could not drive out the people of the valley because they had iron chariots? That failure to finish the conquest sets up the whole book of Judges."],
 questions:[
  {q:"After Joshua died, what was the first question Israel asked the Lord?", options:["Who shall go up for us against the Canaanites first, to fight against them?","Where shall we build the tabernacle?","Who shall be our new leader?","How many cities should we take?"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:1",verse:"Now after the death of Joshua it came to pass, that the children of Israel asked the LORD, saying, Who shall go up for us against the Canaanites first, to fight against them?",
   insight:"Israel's first move after losing Joshua was to ask God for direction, and that instinct to seek Him first is what set the conquest in motion."},
  {q:"Which tribe did the Lord choose to go up first against the Canaanites?", options:["Judah","Simeon","Benjamin","Dan"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:2",verse:"And the LORD said, Judah shall go up: behold, I have delivered the land into his hand.",
   insight:"Judah means praise, and God put the tribe of praise at the front of the battle, a reminder that worship often leads the way into hard fights."},
  {q:"How many Canaanites and Perizzites did Judah slay at Bezek?", options:["Ten thousand","Five thousand","One thousand","Forty thousand"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:4",verse:"And Judah went up; and the LORD delivered the Canaanites and the Perizzites into their hand: and they slew of them in Bezek ten thousand men.",
   insight:"The victory at Bezek was total, ten thousand men, showing that when Judah obeyed, God gave overwhelming success."},
  {q:"What did Judah do to Adonibezek when they captured him?", options:["Cut off his thumbs and his great toes","Blinded him","Hanged him on a tree","Threw him into prison"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:6",verse:"But Adoni-bezek fled; and they pursued after him, and caught him, and cut off his thumbs and his great toes.",
   insight:"The punishment fit the crime exactly, because Adonibezek had done the same to seventy kings, and he admitted God was repaying him."},
  {q:"How many kings did Adonibezek say he had maimed in the same way?", options:["Seventy","Forty","Twelve","One hundred"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:7",verse:"And Adoni-bezek said, Threescore and ten kings, having their thumbs and their great toes cut off, gathered their meat under my table: as I have done, so God hath requited me. And they brought him to Jerusalem, and there he died.",
   insight:"Even a pagan king could see the justice of God: 'as I have done, so God hath requited me.'"},
  {q:"Where was Adonibezek brought to die?", options:["Jerusalem","Bezek","Hebron","Gilgal"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:7",verse:"And Adoni-bezek said, Threescore and ten kings, having their thumbs and their great toes cut off, gathered their meat under my table: as I have done, so God hath requited me. And they brought him to Jerusalem, and there he died.",
   insight:"He was carried to Jerusalem, the very city his power could not protect, and died there under the judgment he had dealt to others."},
  {q:"Which city did the children of Judah fight against and capture?", options:["Jerusalem","Jericho","Ai","Gaza"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:8",verse:"Now the children of Judah had fought against Jerusalem, and had taken it, and smitten it with the edge of the sword, and set the city on fire.",
   insight:"Judah's capture of Jerusalem showed what obedience could win, though the Jebusites would later return to it."},
  {q:"What was the earlier name of Hebron?", options:["Kirjatharba","Kirjathsepher","Luz","Salem"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:10",verse:"And Judah went against the Canaanites that dwelt in Hebron: (now the name of Hebron before was Kirjath-arba:) and they slew Sheshai, and Ahiman, and Talmai.",
   insight:"Hebron was once called Kirjatharba, city of Arba, named for a giant's ancestor, which made Caleb's taking of it all the bolder."},
  {q:"What was the earlier name of Debir?", options:["Kirjathsepher","Kirjatharba","Bethel","Hormah"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:11",verse:"And from thence he went against the inhabitants of Debir: and the name of Debir before was Kirjath-sepher:",
   insight:"Debir's old name, Kirjathsepher, means city of the book, and its capture became Caleb's famous challenge."},
  {q:"What did Caleb promise to whoever captured Kirjathsepher?", options:["His daughter Achsah as wife","Half of his lands","A thousand shekels of silver","The city itself as a possession"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:12",verse:"And Caleb said, He that smiteth Kirjath-sepher, and taketh it, to him will I give Achsah my daughter to wife.",
   insight:"Caleb put his own daughter's hand on the line, which shows how much he valued courage in the next generation."},
  {q:"Who captured Kirjathsepher and married Achsah?", options:["Othniel the son of Kenaz","Ehud the Benjamite","Shamgar","Barak"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:13",verse:"And Othniel the son of Kenaz, Caleb’s younger brother, took it: and he gave him Achsah his daughter to wife.",
   insight:"Othniel, Caleb's younger brother, proved his courage here, and God would later raise him as Israel's first judge."},
  {q:"What did Achsah ask Caleb to give her?", options:["A field","A vineyard","A walled city","Servants"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:14",verse:"And it came to pass, when she came to him, that she moved him to ask of her father a field: and she lighted from off her ass; and Caleb said unto her, What wilt thou?",
   insight:"Achsah was bold enough to ask her father for a field, and bold asking runs in that family."},
  {q:"Besides the field, what springs did Caleb give Achsah?", options:["The upper springs and the nether springs","The springs of En-gedi","The well of Beersheba","The fountains of Gilead"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:15",verse:"And she said unto him, Give me a blessing: for thou hast given me a south land; give me also springs of water. And Caleb gave her the upper springs and the nether springs.",
   insight:"Caleb gave her both the upper and the lower springs, water for every season, a father's generous answer to a daughter's bold request."},
  {q:"Who went up out of the city of palm trees with the children of Judah?", options:["The Kenites","The Jebusites","The Perizzites","The Hivites"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:16",verse:"And the children of the Kenite, Moses’ father in law, went up out of the city of palm trees with the children of Judah into the wilderness of Judah, which lieth in the south of Arad; and they went and dwelt among the people.",
   insight:"The Kenites, Moses' in-laws, chose to dwell with Judah rather than return to comfort, and God honored that loyalty for generations."},
  {q:"Why could Judah not drive out the inhabitants of the valley?", options:["Because they had chariots of iron","Because they were too many to count","Because Judah's men were weary","Because God had forbidden the attack"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:19",verse:"And the LORD was with Judah; and he drave out the inhabitants of the mountain; but could not drive out the inhabitants of the valley, because they had chariots of iron.",
   insight:"Iron chariots looked unbeatable, but the real problem was that Judah stopped trusting the God who had already given them the hill country."},
  {q:"To whom was Hebron given?", options:["Caleb","Othniel","The tribe of Judah","The Levites"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:20",verse:"And they gave Hebron unto Caleb, as Moses said: and he expelled thence the three sons of Anak.",
   insight:"Caleb finally received Hebron, the city he had spied out forty years earlier, a reward for a lifetime of wholehearted faith."},
  {q:"Which tribe failed to drive the Jebusites out of Jerusalem?", options:["Benjamin","Judah","Ephraim","Dan"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:21",verse:"And the children of Benjamin did not drive out the Jebusites that inhabited Jerusalem; but the Jebusites dwell with the children of Benjamin in Jerusalem unto this day.",
   insight:"Benjamin's failure left the Jebusites living side by side with Israel in Jerusalem, and that compromise would fester for centuries."},
  {q:"What did the spies of Joseph ask the man coming out of Luz?", options:["Shew us, we pray thee, the entrance into the city","Where is your king hiding?","How many soldiers are inside?","Will you join our army?"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:24",verse:"And the spies saw a man come forth out of the city, and they said unto him, Shew us, we pray thee, the entrance into the city, and we will shew thee mercy.",
   insight:"Joseph's spies traded mercy for intelligence, and the man's betrayal of his city bought his family's life."},
  {q:"What happened to the man who showed them the entrance to Luz?", options:["They let him go with all his family","They made him their slave","They put him to death","They took him prisoner to Gilgal"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:25",verse:"And when he shewed them the entrance into the city, they smote the city with the edge of the sword; but they let go the man and all his family.",
   insight:"The spies kept their word and spared the man, a small mercy that echoes Rahab's story at Jericho."},
  {q:"Who forced the children of Dan into the mountains?", options:["The Amorites","The Philistines","The Jebusites","The Moabites"], a:0, ref:"Judges 1:34",verse:"And the Amorites forced the children of Dan into the mountain: for they would not suffer them to come down to the valley:",
   insight:"The Amorites pressed Dan back into the hills, another sign that Israel's unfinished conquest was already shrinking their inheritance."}
 ]},
{n:2, name:"Weeping at Bochim", seal:{ref:"Judges 2:10",verse:"And also all that generation were gathered unto their fathers: and there arose another generation after them, which knew not the LORD, nor yet the works which he had done for Israel."},
 facts:["Did you know Bochim means weepers? The place got its name because the whole nation broke down crying when the angel of the Lord rebuked them there.",
 "Did you know Judges 2:10 describes the turning point of an entire generation: there arose another generation which knew not the Lord, nor yet the works which he had done for Israel?",
 "Did you know God deliberately left enemy nations in the land, partly to test whether Israel would obey Him, and partly so the new generation would learn warfare?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"From where did the angel of the Lord come up to Bochim?", options:["Gilgal","Shiloh","Bethel","Jericho"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:1",verse:"And an angel of the LORD came up from Gilgal to Bochim, and said, I made you to go up out of Egypt, and have brought you unto the land which I sware unto your fathers; and I said, I will never break my covenant with you.",
   insight:"The angel came from Gilgal, the place of Israel's first camp and consecration, to confront them with how far they had drifted."},
  {q:"What did the angel say God would never do?", options:["Break His covenant with them","Leave the land of Canaan","Send them another judge","Forgive their idolatry"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:1",verse:"And an angel of the LORD came up from Gilgal to Bochim, and said, I made you to go up out of Egypt, and have brought you unto the land which I sware unto your fathers; and I said, I will never break my covenant with you.",
   insight:"Even in rebuke, God anchored His people in His promise: I will never break my covenant with you."},
  {q:"What two commands had Israel disobeyed?", options:["Make no league with the inhabitants, and throw down their altars","Offer no sacrifices, and build no cities","Pay no tribute, and take no wives","Fight no battles, and keep no Sabbaths"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:2",verse:"And ye shall make no league with the inhabitants of this land; ye shall throw down their altars: but ye have not obeyed my voice: why have ye done this?",
   insight:"God had asked for separation and demolition, no treaties and no altars, and Israel had kept neither command."},
  {q:"What did God say the remaining nations would become to Israel?", options:["Thorns in their sides","A blessing to their children","Teachers of their craftsmen","Servants in their fields"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:3",verse:"Wherefore I also said, I will not drive them out from before you; but they shall be as thorns in your sides, and their gods shall be a snare unto you.",
   insight:"The nations Israel spared became thorns in their sides, exactly the pain God had warned them about."},
  {q:"How did the people respond when the angel finished speaking?", options:["They lifted up their voice, and wept","They offered burnt sacrifices","They marched out to battle","They returned to their tents in silence"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:4",verse:"And it came to pass, when the angel of the LORD spake these words unto all the children of Israel, that the people lifted up their voice, and wept.",
   insight:"The whole congregation wept aloud, which is why the place was named Bochim, weepers."},
  {q:"What did the people call the place where the angel spoke?", options:["Bochim","Gilgal","Mizpeh","Bethel"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:5",verse:"And they called the name of that place Bochim: and they sacrificed there unto the LORD.",
   insight:"They named it Bochim, weepers, so every mention of the place would recall their tears of conviction."},
  {q:"Why did God raise up deliverance for Israel again and again?", options:["Because He pitied their groanings under oppression","Because they had earned it by repentance","Because the judges demanded it","Because the nations feared His name"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:18",verse:"And when the LORD raised them up judges, then the LORD was with the judge, and delivered them out of the hand of their enemies all the days of the judge: for it repented the LORD because of their groanings by reason of them that oppressed them and vexed them.",
   insight:"God's heart moved at their groanings, and He delivered them not because they earned it but because He pitied them."},
  {q:"What did every man do when Joshua dismissed the people?", options:["Went to his own inheritance","Went up to Shiloh","Stayed behind at Gilgal","Returned toward Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:6",verse:"And when Joshua had let the people go, the children of Israel went every man unto his inheritance to possess the land.",
   insight:"Each man went to his own inheritance, settling into the land that would soon test their faithfulness."},
  {q:"How long did Israel serve the Lord?", options:["All the days of Joshua and the elders who outlived him","Only forty years","Until the first judge died","They never served Him fully"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:7",verse:"And the people served the LORD all the days of Joshua, and all the days of the elders that outlived Joshua, who had seen all the great works of the LORD, that he did for Israel.",
   insight:"Faithfulness lasted exactly one generation past Joshua, a sobering picture of how quickly zeal can fade."},
  {q:"How old was Joshua when he died?", options:["One hundred and ten","One hundred and twenty","One hundred","Ninety"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:8",verse:"And Joshua the son of Nun, the servant of the LORD, died, being an hundred and ten years old.",
   insight:"Joshua died at 110, full of years and victories, but his greatest work, passing faith to the next generation, was left unfinished."},
  {q:"Where was Joshua buried?", options:["In Timnathheres in Mount Ephraim","In Shechem","In Hebron","In Gilgal"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:9",verse:"And they buried him in the border of his inheritance in Timnath-heres, in the mount of Ephraim, on the north side of the hill Gaash.",
   insight:"They buried Joshua in his own inheritance at Timnathheres, in the hill country he had fought to win."},
  {q:"What marked the generation that arose after Joshua's elders died?", options:["They knew not the Lord, nor His works for Israel","They were stronger than their fathers","They built the temple","They conquered the Philistines"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:10",verse:"And also all that generation were gathered unto their fathers: and there arose another generation after them, which knew not the LORD, nor yet the works which he had done for Israel.",
   insight:"This is the saddest verse in Judges: a whole generation grew up not knowing the Lord or what He had done."},
  {q:"Whom did Israel serve instead of the Lord?", options:["Baalim","The gods of Egypt","Molech alone","The sun and the moon"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:11",verse:"And the children of Israel did evil in the sight of the LORD, and served Baalim:",
   insight:"They traded the living God for Baalim, the local fertility gods, chasing what their neighbors worshiped."},
  {q:"Whom did Israel forsake?", options:["The Lord God of their fathers, who brought them out of Egypt","Moses their lawgiver","Joshua their captain","The covenant of circumcision"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:12",verse:"And they forsook the LORD God of their fathers, which brought them out of the land of Egypt, and followed other gods, of the gods of the people that were round about them, and bowed themselves unto them, and provoked the LORD to anger.",
   insight:"They forsook the very God who had carried them out of Egypt, the God their fathers had known by name."},
  {q:"Which pair of false gods did Israel serve?", options:["Baal and Ashtaroth","Chemosh and Molech","Dagon and Baal","Ashtaroth and Tammuz"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:13",verse:"And they forsook the LORD, and served Baal and Ashtaroth.",
   insight:"Baal and Ashtaroth, the male and female gods of Canaan, became Israel's new devotion, and their worship dragged the nation into every corruption."},
  {q:"What did God's anger do to Israel?", options:["Delivered them into the hands of spoilers","Sent a famine on the land","Scattered them among the nations","Destroyed the tabernacle"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:14",verse:"And the anger of the LORD was hot against Israel, and he delivered them into the hands of spoilers that spoiled them, and he sold them into the hands of their enemies round about, so that they could not any longer stand before their enemies.",
   insight:"God's anger was not random fury; He handed them to plunderers so they would feel the weight of their choices."},
  {q:"What happened whenever Israel went out to battle?", options:["The hand of the Lord was against them","They always won the day","The judges led them to victory","The ark went before them"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:15",verse:"Whithersoever they went out, the hand of the LORD was against them for evil, as the LORD had said, and as the LORD had sworn unto them: and they were greatly distressed.",
   insight:"The same hand that had fought for Israel now fought against them, because they had walked away from Him."},
  {q:"What did God raise up to deliver Israel?", options:["Judges","Kings","Prophets","Priests"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:16",verse:"Nevertheless the LORD raised up judges, which delivered them out of the hand of those that spoiled them.",
   insight:"Even in judgment, God raised up judges, military deliverers who foreshadowed the greater Deliverer to come."},
  {q:"How did Israel respond to their judges?", options:["They would not listen, but chased after other gods","They obeyed them fully","They made them kings over Israel","They ignored only a few of them"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:17",verse:"And yet they would not hearken unto their judges, but they went a whoring after other gods, and bowed themselves unto them: they turned quickly out of the way which their fathers walked in, obeying the commandments of the LORD; but they did not so.",
   insight:"They would not even listen to the rescuers God sent, preferring the gods that had enslaved them."},
  {q:"What happened each time a judge died?", options:["They returned and corrupted themselves more than their fathers","They kept the peace of the land","They chose a king to rule them","They repented fully and finally"], a:0, ref:"Judges 2:19",verse:"And it came to pass, when the judge was dead, that they returned, and corrupted themselves more than their fathers, in following other gods to serve them, and to bow down unto them; they ceased not from their own doings, nor from their stubborn way.",
   insight:"Every death of a judge brought a deeper relapse, proving that borrowed faith never survives the one who lent it."}
 ]},
{n:3, name:"The Left-Handed Judge", seal:{ref:"Judges 3:31",verse:"And after him was Shamgar the son of Anath, which slew of the Philistines six hundred men with an ox goad: and he also delivered Israel."},
 facts:["Did you know Ehud hid an eighteen inch dagger on his right thigh because, being left-handed, nobody checked that side? The detail is in Judges 3:16.",
 "Did you know Eglon was so fat that when Ehud stabbed him, the whole handle disappeared and the fat closed over the blade?",
 "Did you know Shamgar, the judge after Ehud, killed six hundred Philistines with nothing but an ox goad, a farmer's stick?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"Why did God leave some nations in the land?", options:["To prove Israel, whether they would keep His commandments","Because He had forgotten His promise","To punish the Canaanites later","To give the nations time to repent"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:1",verse:"Now these are the nations which the LORD left, to prove Israel by them, even as many of Israel as had not known all the wars of Canaan;",
   insight:"God left enemies in place as a test, to reveal whether Israel would keep His commandments."},
  {q:"What did the new generation of Israelites need to learn?", options:["War","Farming","Building","Sailing"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:2",verse:"Only that the generations of the children of Israel might know, to teach them war, at the least such as before knew nothing thereof;",
   insight:"A generation that had never fought needed to learn war, because the battles of faith are not won by inheritance."},
  {q:"Among which peoples did the children of Israel dwell?", options:["Canaanites, Hittites, Amorites, Perizzites, Hivites, and Jebusites","Egyptians, Moabites, Ammonites, and Edomites","Philistines only","Midianites and Amalekites"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:5",verse:"And the children of Israel dwelt among the Canaanites, Hittites, and Amorites, and Perizzites, and Hivites, and Jebusites:",
   insight:"Israel settled among six peoples instead of driving them out, and proximity soon became partnership."},
  {q:"What compromises did Israel make with these peoples?", options:["They intermarried with them and served their gods","They traded and farmed alongside them","They learned their language","They paid them yearly tribute"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:6",verse:"And they took their daughters to be their wives, and gave their daughters to their sons, and served their gods.",
   insight:"Marriage led to worship: they took Canaanite spouses, then served Canaanite gods, exactly the slide Moses had warned about."},
  {q:"What did Israel forget?", options:["The Lord their God","The way back to Canaan","The Sabbath day","The Passover feast"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:7",verse:"And the children of Israel did evil in the sight of the LORD, and forgat the LORD their God, and served Baalim and the groves.",
   insight:"They forgot the Lord their God, and forgetting is always the first step toward idolatry."},
  {q:"How many years did Israel serve Chushanrishathaim king of Mesopotamia?", options:["Eight","Twelve","Eighteen","Forty"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:8",verse:"Therefore the anger of the LORD was hot against Israel, and he sold them into the hand of Chushan-rishathaim king of Mesopotamia: and the children of Israel served Chushan-rishathaim eight years.",
   insight:"Eight years of bondage to a distant king was the price of forgetting God, and it was enough to make them cry out."},
  {q:"Who was the first judge God raised up?", options:["Othniel the son of Kenaz","Ehud the Benjamite","Shamgar","Gideon"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:9",verse:"And when the children of Israel cried unto the LORD, the LORD raised up a deliverer to the children of Israel, who delivered them, even Othniel the son of Kenaz, Caleb’s younger brother.",
   insight:"Othniel, the hero of Kirjathsepher, became Israel's first deliverer, proof that courage in small battles prepares a man for greater ones."},
  {q:"What came upon Othniel when he judged Israel?", options:["The Spirit of the Lord","A mighty hired army","The wisdom of old age","A prophetic dream"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:10",verse:"And the Spirit of the LORD came upon him, and he judged Israel, and went out to war: and the LORD delivered Chushan-rishathaim king of Mesopotamia into his hand; and his hand prevailed against Chushan-rishathaim.",
   insight:"The Spirit of the Lord came upon Othniel, and every true deliverance in Judges begins with that same empowering."},
  {q:"How long did the land rest under Othniel?", options:["Forty years","Twenty years","Eighty years","Seven years"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:11",verse:"And the land had rest forty years. And Othniel the son of Kenaz died.",
   insight:"Forty years of rest followed Othniel's victory, showing how much peace one obedient life can bring a nation."},
  {q:"Which king did God strengthen against Israel after they sinned again?", options:["Eglon king of Moab","Jabin king of Hazor","The king of Midian","The lords of the Philistines"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:12",verse:"And the children of Israel did evil again in the sight of the LORD: and the LORD strengthened Eglon the king of Moab against Israel, because they had done evil in the sight of the LORD.",
   insight:"God strengthened Eglon of Moab against them, using Israel's enemies as instruments of discipline."},
  {q:"Which two peoples joined Eglon against Israel?", options:["The Ammonites and the Amalekites","The Philistines and the Midianites","The Edomites and the Moabites","The Hittites and the Jebusites"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:13",verse:"And he gathered unto him the children of Ammon and Amalek, and went and smote Israel, and possessed the city of palm trees.",
   insight:"Eglon gathered Ammon and Amalek, old enemies of Israel, and together they seized the city of palm trees."},
  {q:"How long did Israel serve Eglon?", options:["Eighteen years","Eight years","Twenty years","Forty years"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:14",verse:"So the children of Israel served Eglon the king of Moab eighteen years.",
   insight:"Eighteen years under a Moabite king wore Israel down until they finally cried out to the Lord again."},
  {q:"What was unusual about Ehud, the deliverer God raised up?", options:["He was left-handed","He was blind","He was only a child","He was a foreigner"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:15",verse:"But when the children of Israel cried unto the LORD, the LORD raised them up a deliverer, Ehud the son of Gera, a Benjamite, a man lefthanded: and by him the children of Israel sent a present unto Eglon the king of Moab.",
   insight:"Ehud was left-handed, a detail that mattered, because guards checked the left thigh for weapons, not the right."},
  {q:"Where did Ehud hide his dagger?", options:["Girded under his clothing on his right thigh","Inside his sandal","Within his staff","Under his cloak"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:16",verse:"But Ehud made him a dagger which had two edges, of a cubit length; and he did gird it under his raiment upon his right thigh.",
   insight:"He strapped a dagger of a cubit length to his right thigh, the one place a left-handed man's weapon would never be expected."},
  {q:"How does Scripture describe Eglon?", options:["A very fat man","A tall warrior","A young prince","A wise elder"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:17",verse:"And he brought the present unto Eglon king of Moab: and Eglon was a very fat man.",
   insight:"Eglon was a very fat man, a picture of a king grown soft on tribute while his servants did his watching."},
  {q:"What words made Eglon rise from his seat?", options:["I have a message from God unto thee","The Lord demands your surrender","Israel sends you greetings","Your servants are waiting outside"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:20",verse:"And Ehud came unto him; and he was sitting in a summer parlour, which he had for himself alone. And Ehud said, I have a message from God unto thee. And he arose out of his seat.",
   insight:"Even a pagan king stood when he heard a message from God, a flicker of reverence in a corrupt court."},
  {q:"Where did Ehud thrust the dagger?", options:["Into his belly","Into his heart","Into his throat","Into his side"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:21",verse:"And Ehud put forth his left hand, and took the dagger from his right thigh, and thrust it into his belly:",
   insight:"Ehud drove the blade into Eglon's belly with his left hand, and the tyrant who had oppressed Israel for eighteen years fell in a moment."},
  {q:"What happened to the dagger after Ehud struck?", options:["The handle went in too, and the fat closed over the blade","It broke in two","It fell to the floor","The guards seized it"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:22",verse:"And the haft also went in after the blade; and the fat closed upon the blade, so that he could not draw the dagger out of his belly; and the dirt came out.",
   insight:"The whole dagger disappeared into Eglon's fat, a grim detail Scripture records to show the completeness of the judgment."},
  {q:"How did Ehud escape the palace?", options:["He passed beyond the quarries while the servants waited","He fought his way past the guards","He hid on the roof","He bribed the doorkeeper"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:26",verse:"And Ehud escaped while they tarried, and passed beyond the quarries, and escaped unto Seirath.",
   insight:"Ehud slipped past the quarries while the servants awkwardly waited outside, and their delay became his escape."},
  {q:"How many Philistines did Shamgar kill, and with what?", options:["Six hundred, with an ox goad","A thousand, with a jawbone","Ten thousand, with a sword","Three hundred, with a spear"], a:0, ref:"Judges 3:31",verse:"And after him was Shamgar the son of Anath, which slew of the Philistines six hundred men with an ox goad: and he also delivered Israel.",
   insight:"Shamgar slew six hundred men with a farmer's ox goad, proving God needs no armies, only willing hands."}
 ]},
{n:4, name:"Deborah, Barak, and Jael", seal:{ref:"Judges 4:21",verse:"Then Jael Heber’s wife took a nail of the tent, and took an hammer in her hand, and went softly unto him, and smote the nail into his temples, and fastened it into the ground: for he was fast asleep and weary. So he died."},
 facts:["Did you know Deborah held court under a palm tree between Ramah and Bethel, and Israelites came up to her there for judgment? She is the only judge described as a prophetess.",
 "Did you know Barak refused to march without Deborah, and she warned him the honor of killing Sisera would go to a woman instead?",
 "Did you know Sisera's nine hundred iron chariots were useless in the muddy Kishon valley, and he fled the battle on foot to Jael's tent?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"When did Israel do evil again?", options:["After Ehud was dead","During Deborah's rule","While Joshua still lived","Before they entered Canaan"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:1",verse:"And the children of Israel again did evil in the sight of the LORD, when Ehud was dead.",
   insight:"The cycle restarted the moment Ehud died, because Israel's repentance had been tied to the man, not to God."},
  {q:"Who was Jabin?", options:["King of Canaan, who reigned in Hazor","A lord of the Philistines","A chief of Midian","A governor of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:2",verse:"And the LORD sold them into the hand of Jabin king of Canaan, that reigned in Hazor; the captain of whose host was Sisera, which dwelt in Harosheth of the Gentiles.",
   insight:"Jabin ruled from Hazor, the great northern stronghold Joshua had once burned, now rebuilt and defiant."},
  {q:"Where did Sisera, Jabin's captain, live?", options:["In Harosheth of the Gentiles","In Hazor","In Megiddo","In Jericho"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:2",verse:"And the LORD sold them into the hand of Jabin king of Canaan, that reigned in Hazor; the captain of whose host was Sisera, which dwelt in Harosheth of the Gentiles.",
   insight:"Sisera commanded from Harosheth of the Gentiles, the headquarters of the most feared army in Canaan."},
  {q:"How many chariots of iron did Sisera have?", options:["Nine hundred","Three hundred","One thousand","One hundred"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:3",verse:"And the children of Israel cried unto the LORD: for he had nine hundred chariots of iron; and twenty years he mightily oppressed the children of Israel.",
   insight:"Nine hundred iron chariots made Sisera's army look invincible, which is exactly why God chose to defeat it."},
  {q:"How long did Jabin and Sisera oppress Israel?", options:["Twenty years","Eight years","Eighteen years","Forty years"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:3",verse:"And the children of Israel cried unto the LORD: for he had nine hundred chariots of iron; and twenty years he mightily oppressed the children of Israel.",
   insight:"Twenty years of cruel oppression finally drove Israel to cry out, and God heard them."},
  {q:"Who was Deborah?", options:["A prophetess, the wife of Lapidoth","A queen of Israel","A daughter of Caleb","A woman of Midian"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:4",verse:"And Deborah, a prophetess, the wife of Lapidoth, she judged Israel at that time.",
   insight:"Deborah was both prophetess and judge, the only woman to hold that office, and God used her to shame a timid generation of men."},
  {q:"Where did Deborah judge Israel?", options:["Under the palm tree of Deborah, between Ramah and Bethel","At the tabernacle in Shiloh","In the gates of Jericho","On the top of Mount Tabor"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:5",verse:"And she dwelt under the palm tree of Deborah between Ramah and Bethel in mount Ephraim: and the children of Israel came up to her for judgment.",
   insight:"She held court under her own palm tree, and the people came up to her there, a public ministry of wisdom and justice."},
  {q:"Whom did Deborah summon to lead Israel's army?", options:["Barak the son of Abinoam","Gideon the son of Joash","Othniel the son of Kenaz","Jephthah the Gileadite"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:6",verse:"And she sent and called Barak the son of Abinoam out of Kedesh-naphtali, and said unto him, Hath not the LORD God of Israel commanded, saying, Go and draw toward mount Tabor, and take with thee ten thousand men of the children of Naphtali and of the children of Zebulun?",
   insight:"Deborah called Barak from Kedeshnaphtali with a direct word from the Lord, giving him both the command and the promise."},
  {q:"How many men was Barak to take to Mount Tabor?", options:["Ten thousand","Thirty thousand","Three hundred","Forty thousand"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:6",verse:"And she sent and called Barak the son of Abinoam out of Kedesh-naphtali, and said unto him, Hath not the LORD God of Israel commanded, saying, Go and draw toward mount Tabor, and take with thee ten thousand men of the children of Naphtali and of the children of Zebulun?",
   insight:"Ten thousand men from Naphtali and Zebulun against nine hundred chariots: God's math has never depended on superior numbers."},
  {q:"Where did God promise to deliver Sisera into Barak's hand?", options:["At the river Kishon","At Mount Tabor","In the valley of Jezreel","At Megiddo"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:7",verse:"And I will draw unto thee to the river Kishon Sisera, the captain of Jabin’s army, with his chariots and his multitude; and I will deliver him into thine hand.",
   insight:"God named the battlefield in advance, the river Kishon, where He would turn Sisera's greatest strength into his ruin."},
  {q:"What condition did Barak set before he would go?", options:["Deborah must go with him","The ark must go before them","Gideon must join the army","The elders must approve the plan"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:8",verse:"And Barak said unto her, If thou wilt go with me, then I will go: but if thou wilt not go with me, then I will not go.",
   insight:"Barak's courage failed at the crucial moment, and his demand revealed a faith that leaned on Deborah instead of on God."},
  {q:"What did Deborah prophesy about the coming victory?", options:["The Lord would sell Sisera into the hand of a woman","Barak would fall in battle","The chariots would be burned with fire","Israel would lose but survive"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:9",verse:"And she said, I will surely go with thee: notwithstanding the journey that thou takest shall not be for thine honour; for the LORD shall sell Sisera into the hand of a woman. And Deborah arose, and went with Barak to Kedesh.",
   insight:"Because Barak hesitated, the honor of the victory would go to a woman, and God kept that word through Jael."},
  {q:"Which tribes did Barak call to Kedesh?", options:["Zebulun and Naphtali","Judah and Simeon","Ephraim and Manasseh","Gad and Reuben"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:10",verse:"And Barak called Zebulun and Naphtali to Kedesh; and he went up with ten thousand men at his feet: and Deborah went up with him.",
   insight:"Barak gathered Zebulun and Naphtali, the northern tribes who lived under Sisera's shadow, to face their oppressor."},
  {q:"Who was Heber the Kenite?", options:["A Kenite who had separated from his people and pitched his tent near Kedesh","A captain in Jabin's army","A prophet of the Lord","A brother of Barak"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:11",verse:"Now Heber the Kenite, which was of the children of Hobab the father in law of Moses, had severed himself from the Kenites, and pitched his tent unto the plain of Zaanaim, which is by Kedesh.",
   insight:"Heber's family had ties to both Israel and Jabin's side, and his tent would become the stage for Sisera's end."},
  {q:"What did Deborah say when Sisera's army appeared?", options:["Up; for this is the day in which the Lord hath delivered Sisera into thine hand","Flee to the mountains","Wait for a sign from heaven","Sound the trumpet"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:14",verse:"And Deborah said unto Barak, Up; for this is the day in which the LORD hath delivered Sisera into thine hand: is not the LORD gone out before thee? So Barak went down from mount Tabor, and ten thousand men after him.",
   insight:"Deborah's battle cry was pure faith: the victory was already decided, Barak only had to march into it."},
  {q:"What happened to Sisera's army at the Kishon?", options:["The Lord discomfited them before Barak","They retreated in good order","They surrendered to Israel","They crossed the river safely"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:15",verse:"And the LORD discomfited Sisera, and all his chariots, and all his host, with the edge of the sword before Barak; so that Sisera lighted down off his chariot, and fled away on his feet.",
   insight:"God Himself threw Sisera's host into panic, and Israel's swords only finished what heaven had started."},
  {q:"Where did Sisera flee after the battle?", options:["To the tent of Jael the wife of Heber","Back to Hazor","Into the mountains","Down to the sea"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:17",verse:"Howbeit Sisera fled away on his feet to the tent of Jael the wife of Heber the Kenite: for there was peace between Jabin the king of Hazor and the house of Heber the Kenite.",
   insight:"Sisera ran on foot to Jael's tent, trusting an old alliance, and walked straight into the prophecy Deborah had spoken."},
  {q:"What did Jael give Sisera when he asked for water?", options:["Milk","Wine","Water","Bread"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:19",verse:"And he said unto her, Give me, I pray thee, a little water to drink; for I am thirsty. And she opened a bottle of milk, and gave him drink, and covered him.",
   insight:"She gave him milk in a lordly dish, lulling the exhausted captain into the sleep from which he would never wake."},
  {q:"What did Sisera ask Jael to do?", options:["Stand in the tent door and deny anyone was inside","Hide his armor","Send a runner to Jabin","Saddle a horse for him"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:20",verse:"Again he said unto her, Stand in the door of the tent, and it shall be, when any man doth come and enquire of thee, and say, Is there any man here? that thou shalt say, No.",
   insight:"Sisera's last order was a lie for Jael to tell, and it was the last word he ever spoke."},
  {q:"How did Jael kill Sisera?", options:["She drove a tent nail through his temples with a hammer","She poisoned his milk","She stabbed him with a sword","She smothered him in his sleep"], a:0, ref:"Judges 4:21",verse:"Then Jael Heber’s wife took a nail of the tent, and took an hammer in her hand, and went softly unto him, and smote the nail into his temples, and fastened it into the ground: for he was fast asleep and weary. So he died.",
   insight:"Jael took a tent peg and hammer and drove it through his temples as he slept, fulfilling Deborah's word that a woman would take the honor."}
 ]},
{n:5, name:"The Song of Deborah", seal:{ref:"Judges 5:31",verse:"So let all thine enemies perish, O LORD: but let them that love him be as the sun when he goeth forth in his might. And the land had rest forty years."},
 facts:["Did you know the Song of Deborah names and shames the tribes that stayed home? Reuben stayed with his sheep, Dan stayed in his ships, and Asher stayed on the seashore.",
 "Did you know the song says the stars themselves fought against Sisera? A sudden storm likely turned the Kishon into a flood that swept away his chariots.",
 "Did you know the song ends with a prayer every believer can echo: So let all thine enemies perish, O Lord?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"Who sang the victory song after Sisera's defeat?", options:["Deborah and Barak","Miriam and Moses","David and Jonathan","Hannah and Samuel"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:1",verse:"Then sang Deborah and Barak the son of Abinoam on that day, saying,",
   insight:"Deborah and Barak sang together on the day of victory, turning a battlefield into a choir."},
  {q:"What does the song praise the Lord for?", options:["The people willingly offering themselves","The early rain","The plentiful harvest","The house of God"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:2",verse:"Praise ye the LORD for the avenging of Israel, when the people willingly offered themselves.",
   insight:"The song begins by praising God for willing hearts, because victory belongs to those who volunteer for it."},
  {q:"Whom does Deborah address in the song?", options:["Kings and princes","The priests of the Lord","The little children","The angels of heaven"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:3",verse:"Hear, O ye kings; give ear, O ye princes; I, even I, will sing unto the LORD; I will sing praise to the LORD God of Israel.",
   insight:"She summons kings and princes to listen, because what God did at the Kishon was news for every throne on earth."},
  {q:"What happened when the Lord went out of Seir?", options:["The earth trembled, and the heavens dropped","The sun stood still","The sea was parted","Fire fell from heaven"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:4",verse:"LORD, when thou wentest out of Seir, when thou marchedst out of the field of Edom, the earth trembled, and the heavens dropped, the clouds also dropped water.",
   insight:"Deborah recalls God marching from Seir with earthquake and storm, the same God who now marched for Israel."},
  {q:"What did the mountains do before the Lord?", options:["They melted","They sang for joy","They moved aside","They burned with fire"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:5",verse:"The mountains melted from before the LORD, even that Sinai from before the LORD God of Israel.",
   insight:"Mountains melted like wax before Him, a picture of how every obstacle dissolves in God's presence."},
  {q:"What were the highways like in the days of Shamgar and Jael?", options:["Unoccupied","Full of merchants","Heavily guarded","Newly built"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:6",verse:"In the days of Shamgar the son of Anath, in the days of Jael, the highways were unoccupied, and the travelers walked through byways.",
   insight:"The roads stood empty because travelers feared ambush, a portrait of a nation too broken to move freely."},
  {q:"What does Deborah call herself in the song?", options:["A mother in Israel","A queen over Israel","A warrior of Israel","The prophetess of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:7",verse:"The inhabitants of the villages ceased, they ceased in Israel, until that I Deborah arose, that I arose a mother in Israel.",
   insight:"A mother in Israel is how Deborah saw herself, a leader who fought not for glory but for her people's life."},
  {q:"What followed when Israel chose new gods?", options:["War in the gates","Lasting peace","Great prosperity","Abundant rain"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:8",verse:"They chose new gods; then was war in the gates: was there a shield or spear seen among forty thousand in Israel?",
   insight:"New gods brought war to the very gates, because idolatry always invites the enemies it promises to keep away."},
  {q:"Where would the people rehearse the righteous acts of the Lord?", options:["In the places of drawing water","In the house of God","On the tops of the mountains","In the gates of the city"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:11",verse:"They that are delivered from the noise of archers in the places of drawing water, there shall they rehearse the righteous acts of the LORD, even the righteous acts toward the inhabitants of his villages in Israel: then shall the people of the LORD go down to the gates.",
   insight:"The song imagines villagers telling God's deeds at the wells, where ordinary life and worship met."},
  {q:"What is Deborah urged to do in the song?", options:["Awake, awake, and utter a song","Sleep, and take her rest","Flee, and hide herself","Weep, and mourn the fallen"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:12",verse:"Awake, awake, Deborah: awake, awake, utter a song: arise, Barak, and lead thy captivity captive, thou son of Abinoam.",
   insight:"Awake, awake, Deborah is the song's own call to worship, rousing the singer to celebrate what God has done."},
  {q:"From which tribe came a root of fighters against Amalek?", options:["Ephraim","Judah","Dan","Gad"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:14",verse:"Out of Ephraim was there a root of them against Amalek; after thee, Benjamin, among thy people; out of Machir came down governors, and out of Zebulun they that handle the pen of the writer.",
   insight:"Ephraim sent its root against Amalek, showing that even old enemies could be faced when the tribes united."},
  {q:"Which tribe's princes stood with Deborah?", options:["Issachar","Reuben","Asher","Gad"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:15",verse:"And the princes of Issachar were with Deborah; even Issachar, and also Barak: he was sent on foot into the valley. For the divisions of Reuben there were great thoughts of heart.",
   insight:"The princes of Issachar stood with Deborah, men of understanding who knew what Israel ought to do."},
  {q:"Why is Reuben rebuked in the song?", options:["He stayed among the sheepfolds, listening to the flocks","He fled toward Egypt","He worshiped Baal with the Canaanites","He attacked his own brothers"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:16",verse:"Why abodest thou among the sheepfolds, to hear the bleatings of the flocks? For the divisions of Reuben there were great searchings of heart.",
   insight:"Reuben stayed home with his sheep while his brothers bled, and the song preserves his shame as a warning."},
  {q:"Which tribe is asked why it remained in ships?", options:["Dan","Asher","Zebulun","Naphtali"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:17",verse:"Gilead abode beyond Jordan: and why did Dan remain in ships? Asher continued on the sea shore, and abode in his breaches.",
   insight:"Dan lingered in his ships, choosing trade over battle, and the song asks the question every comfortable believer must face."},
  {q:"Where did the kings come and fight?", options:["At Taanach, by the waters of Megiddo","At Jericho","At Hazor","At Gilgal"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:19",verse:"The kings came and fought, then fought the kings of Canaan in Taanach by the waters of Megiddo; they took no gain of money.",
   insight:"The kings massed at Taanach by Megiddo's waters, the great battlefield of the north, and there God broke them."},
  {q:"Who fought against Sisera from heaven?", options:["The stars in their courses","The angels with drawn swords","The storm clouds","The rushing winds"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:20",verse:"They fought from heaven; the stars in their courses fought against Sisera.",
   insight:"The stars in their courses fought against Sisera, heaven itself enlisted in Israel's cause."},
  {q:"Which river swept the enemy away?", options:["The Kishon","The Jordan","The Jabbok","The Euphrates"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:21",verse:"The river of Kishon swept them away, that ancient river, the river Kishon. O my soul, thou hast trodden down strength.",
   insight:"The Kishon, swollen by God's storm, swept away the chariots that had terrorized Israel for twenty years."},
  {q:"Why was Meroz cursed?", options:["They did not come to the help of the Lord","They worshiped Baal","They betrayed Barak","They fled from the battle"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:23",verse:"Curse ye Meroz, said the angel of the LORD, curse ye bitterly the inhabitants thereof; because they came not to the help of the LORD, to the help of the LORD against the mighty.",
   insight:"Meroz was cursed not for fighting against God but for doing nothing, a solemn warning against neutrality."},
  {q:"Who is called blessed above women in the song?", options:["Jael the wife of Heber","Deborah the prophetess","Miriam the sister of Moses","Rahab of Jericho"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:24",verse:"Blessed above women shall Jael the wife of Heber the Kenite be, blessed shall she be above women in the tent.",
   insight:"Jael is blessed above women, the tent-dwelling wife whose courage finished what an army began."},
  {q:"What did the mother of Sisera do while waiting for him?", options:["Looked out the window and cried, Why is his chariot so long in coming?","Sent messengers to find him","Prepared a victory feast","Fled from the city"], a:0, ref:"Judges 5:28",verse:"The mother of Sisera looked out at a window, and cried through the lattice, Why is his chariot so long in coming? why tarry the wheels of his chariots?",
   insight:"Sisera's mother watched the road for a son who would never return, a heartbreaking picture of pride's empty wait."}
 ]}
]);
